&lt;p class=MsoNormal align=center style='margin-right:-.5in;text-align:center'>&lt;u>&lt;span
style='font-size:12.0pt'>Basic Structure&lt;/span>&lt;/u>&lt;/p>
&lt;p class=MsoNormal align=center style='margin-right:-.5in;text-align:center'>&lt;u>&lt;span
style='font-size:12.0pt'>&lt;span style='text-decoration:none'>&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/u>&lt;/p>
&lt;p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:-.5in;text-align:justify'>&lt;span
style='font-size:12.0pt'>         This chapter sketches the original organization
of the church in the New Testament. It determines what positions exercised
authority and which were functions, all being places of service. The procedure works
because it addresses an either-or matter and because authority includes
function. We cannot suppose that a &lt;u>function&lt;/u> seemed to be such only
because the records happen to be insufficient to show that it was really an &lt;u>office&lt;/u>.
That would argue from silence and shift the burden of proof onto the negative. The
negative burden would be impossible since the only available evidence would
pertain to function, which is part of the larger concept: office.&lt;/span>&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>SUMMARY OF OFFICES IN THE CHURCH</title><link>https://christir.org/essays/topics/christian-doctrine/church-ecclesiology/summary-of-offices-in-the-church/</link><pubDate>Thu, 02 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://christir.org/essays/topics/christian-doctrine/church-ecclesiology/summary-of-offices-in-the-church/</guid><description>&lt;style>
